g, at move 47, left Black ahead by 12.3, and Opus 5.5 resigned from there.", "moments": [{"turn": 19, "text": "Black's H2 cost 16.4 points and turned a +15.6 lead into -0.8, so White was suddenly slightly ahead. It was the 12k's first big slip, and it came right after Opus 5.5's own 13.9-point loss at H3."}, {"turn": 20, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with H5, which lost 27.4 points and sent the lead from -0.8 back to +26.5 for Black. The engine's choice was F2, and White's chance was gone within one move."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "D5 lost 31.5 points, moving the lead from +2.5 to +34.0 for Black. The 12k's D6 then lost 30.4 points and pulled it back to +3.6. Two moves, one huge swing each way, and the game was still open."}, {"turn": 46, "text": "J8 lost 33.0 points, the worst move of Opus 5.5's game, lifting Black's lead from +12.0 to +45.0. The engine preferred J3. The move gave Black the largest lead of the whole game."}, {"turn": 47, "text": "The 12k answered with H1, which lost 32.6 points and dropped the lead from +45.0 to +12.4. That was Black's worst move of the game, and it gave back almost everything. Black still held +12.3 at the end, enough for Opus 5.5 to resign."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py applies one named move, removes captures and prints each group's liberties; libs.py prints liberties for the pasted position; count.py counts stones and territory. None of them loops over candidate moves, scores them, or plays out continuations, so they only inspect the current position.", "stop": false}}